# Auth Feature Architecture Context

## Purpose
This file captures the current architecture contract for `features/auth` so future prompts can use it as direct context without re-analyzing the module.

## Scope
- Feature root: `src/main/java/com/veterinaria/consentidos/features/auth`
- Public HTTP base path: `/api/auth` from `AuthController`
- Current status: login, refresh, and logout are implemented with Google ID tokens, JWT access/refresh tokens, Redis-backed session/attempt stores, and PostgreSQL-backed auth users in non-local profiles.
- Local profile supports an opt-in `external-storage` override to run PostgreSQL + Redis in local too.

## Architectural Style
- Vertical slice by feature (`auth`)
- Layered + ports/adapters (hexagonal influence)
- Dependency direction:
  - `presentation -> application -> domain`
  - `infrastructure` implements contracts declared by `domain`
  - `domain` does not depend on Spring or infrastructure details

## Module Structure and Responsibilities

### 1) presentation
Path: `presentation/controller`, `presentation/dto`

Responsibilities:
- Expose HTTP endpoints under `/api/auth`
- Receive request DTOs and return response DTOs
- Validate incoming data and delegate to use cases
- Keep business rules out of controllers

Current artifacts:
- `presentation/controller/AuthController`
- `presentation/dto/request/LoginRequestDto`
- `presentation/dto/request/RefreshTokenRequestDto`
- `presentation/dto/request/LogoutRequestDto`
- `presentation/dto/response/AuthTokenResponseDto`
- `presentation/dto/response/LogoutResponseDto`

Current endpoints:
- `POST /api/auth/login`
- `POST /api/auth/refresh`
- `POST /api/auth/logout`

Notes:
- `AuthController` maps request DTOs to application commands
- Login reads `X-Device-Id` and request metadata for login attempt tracking
- `AuthController` returns DTOs directly; cross-cutting response wrapping is handled by global middleware

### 2) application
Path: `application/command`, `application/usecase`

Responsibilities:
- Define input commands for auth flows
- Orchestrate authentication and session lifecycle
- Coordinate domain entities and repository ports
- Enforce use-case level rules

Current artifacts:

Commands:
- `application/command/LoginWithGoogleCommand`
- `application/command/RefreshSessionCommand`
- `application/command/LogoutCommand`

Use cases:
- `application/usecase/LoginWithGoogleUseCase`
- `application/usecase/RefreshSessionUseCase`
- `application/usecase/LogoutUseCase`

Behavior:
- `LoginWithGoogleUseCase`
  - verifies a Google `id_token`
  - checks the configured Google domain allow-list
  - applies login throttling and lockout state through `LoginAttemptRepository`
  - finds an active pre-registered/user-row by Google subject
  - revokes the previous active session for that user
  - creates access and refresh JWTs
  - persists refresh session state
  - clears login-attempt state on success
- `RefreshSessionUseCase`
  - parses and validates the refresh token
  - checks token session state
  - ensures the token is still the active session for that user
  - revokes rotated/expired tokens
  - issues a new token pair
- `LogoutUseCase`
  - parses the refresh token
  - revokes the corresponding refresh session

### 3) domain
Path: `domain/entity`, `domain/repository`

Responsibilities:
- Hold auth-related domain models and invariants
- Declare repository and service ports
- Remain framework-agnostic

Current entities:
- `domain/entity/AuthUser`
- `domain/entity/GoogleIdentity`
- `domain/entity/RefreshTokenSession`
- `domain/entity/TokenPair`
- `domain/entity/LoginAttemptState`
- `domain/entity/LoginAttemptPolicy`

Current repository/service ports:
- `domain/repository/AuthUserRepository`
- `domain/repository/GoogleIdentityVerifier`
- `domain/repository/JwtTokenService`
- `domain/repository/RefreshTokenSessionRepository`
- `domain/repository/LoginAttemptRepository`

Domain model notes:
- `AuthUser` represents a local authenticated/pre-registered user
- The canonical user identifier is now a UUID string (`userId`)
- `RefreshTokenSession` tracks refresh token lifecycle and revocation state
- `TokenPair` models issued access/refresh token output
- `LoginAttemptState` tracks throttling state, window start, lockout, and metadata for login attempts

### 4) infrastructure
Path: `infrastructure/config`, `infrastructure/persistence/*`

Responsibilities:
- Provide concrete implementations of domain ports
- Wire technical configuration and external integrations
- Handle token signing/verification and persistence

Current config classes:
- `infrastructure/config/AuthTokenProperties`
- `infrastructure/config/GoogleAuthProperties`
- `infrastructure/config/RedisAuthProperties`
- `infrastructure/config/AuthLoginAttemptProperties`
- `infrastructure/config/LoginAttemptPolicyConfiguration`
- `infrastructure/config/AuthRedisConfiguration`
- `infrastructure/config/PreRegisteredUsersProperties`

Current persistence adapters:
- `infrastructure/persistence/adapter/GoogleIdentityVerifierAdapter`
- `infrastructure/persistence/adapter/PostgresAuthUserRepositoryAdapter`
- `infrastructure/persistence/adapter/RedisRefreshTokenSessionRepositoryAdapter`
- `infrastructure/persistence/adapter/RedisLoginAttemptRepositoryAdapter`
- `infrastructure/persistence/adapter/InMemoryAuthUserRepositoryAdapter`
- `infrastructure/persistence/adapter/InMemoryRefreshTokenSessionRepositoryAdapter`
- `infrastructure/persistence/adapter/InMemoryLoginAttemptRepositoryAdapter`
- `infrastructure/persistence/adapter/JwtTokenServiceAdapter`

Current persistence substructure:
- `entity`: JPA auth-user entity
- `repository`: Spring Data JPA repository for auth users
- `adapter`: concrete implementations of domain ports

### Infrastructure behavior

#### `GoogleIdentityVerifierAdapter`
- Uses Google verifier APIs to validate `id_token`
- Converts Google payload into `GoogleIdentity`
- Checks allowed Google domains when the allow-list is configured
- Throws `SecurityException` on invalid or failed verification

#### `PostgresAuthUserRepositoryAdapter`
- Reads active auth users from PostgreSQL
- Maps the DB row to the domain `AuthUser`
- Supports lookup by Google subject and by user id

#### `RedisRefreshTokenSessionRepositoryAdapter`
- Stores refresh token sessions in Redis with TTL
- Maintains the active refresh token per user for single-session enforcement
- Supports save, lookup, and revoke
- Revocation updates `revokedAt` and `revokedReason`

#### `RedisLoginAttemptRepositoryAdapter`
- Stores login-attempt counters and lockout metadata in Redis with TTL
- Tracks attempts, window start, last attempt, device id, and hashed request metadata
- Supports save, lookup, and clear

#### `InMemoryAuthUserRepositoryAdapter`
- Loads pre-registered users from `PreRegisteredUsersProperties`
- Stores them in memory for the `local` profile
- Supports lookup by Google subject and by user id
- Returns only active users

#### `InMemoryRefreshTokenSessionRepositoryAdapter`
- Stores refresh token sessions in memory for the `local` profile
- Supports save, lookup, active-session lookup, and revoke
- Revocation updates `revokedAt` and `revokedReason`

#### `InMemoryLoginAttemptRepositoryAdapter`
- Stores login-attempt state in memory for the `local` profile
- Supports save, lookup, and clear

#### `JwtTokenServiceAdapter`
- Creates RSA-signed JWT access tokens and refresh tokens
- Parses refresh tokens and validates issuer/signature/type
- Uses `AuthTokenProperties` for issuer, audience, TTLs, and RSA key material

## Request/Response and Cross-cutting Contracts

### Global response shape
`core/middleware/ResponseInterceptor` wraps non-string successful responses with:
- `status`
- `success`
- `message`
- `data`
- `path`
- `timestamp`

Implication for Auth:
- Controllers can return DTOs directly; the interceptor standardizes the response envelope.

### Global error shape
`core/middleware/ErrorHandler` handles validation/runtime/general exceptions with standard keys like:
- `timestamp`
- `status`
- `error`
- `message`
- `path`
- `errors` for validation cases

Implication for Auth:
- Use cases and adapters can throw meaningful exceptions; global handling formats them.

### Correlation and logging
`core/middleware/RequestInterceptor`:
- Reads or generates `X-Correlation-ID`
- Adds it to response headers
- Logs request start/completion and execution time

Implication for Auth:
- Keep logs correlation-aware in auth flows and external calls.

## End-to-end Flow
1. Client calls `/api/auth/login`, `/api/auth/refresh`, or `/api/auth/logout`
2. Controller validates request DTO and builds a command
3. Use case executes auth logic
4. Use case invokes repository/service ports
5. Infrastructure adapter performs verification, JWT signing, Redis persistence, or PostgreSQL lookup
6. Use case returns a result DTO/domain view
7. `ResponseInterceptor` wraps successful responses
8. `ErrorHandler` formats failures if exceptions occur

## Current Auth Flow Details

### Login with Google
- Input: Google `id_token`
- Verification: `GoogleIdentityVerifier`
- Throttling: `LoginAttemptRepository`
- User lookup: `AuthUserRepository.findActiveByGoogleSub`
- Single-session enforcement: `RefreshTokenSessionRepository.revokeByUserId`
- Token generation: `JwtTokenService.createAccessToken` and `createRefreshToken`
- Session persistence: `RefreshTokenSessionRepository.save`
- Success cleanup: `LoginAttemptRepository.clear`

### Refresh session
- Input: refresh token
- Parsing: `JwtTokenService.parseRefreshToken`
- Session lookup: `RefreshTokenSessionRepository.findByTokenId`
- Active session verification: `RefreshTokenSessionRepository.findActiveByUserId`
- Revocation on expired or rotated token
- New token pair issuance with a new refresh token id

### Logout
- Input: refresh token
- Parsing: `JwtTokenService.parseRefreshToken`
- Revocation: `RefreshTokenSessionRepository.revoke`

## Current Token Strategy
- JWT is used for both access and refresh tokens
- Tokens are RSA-signed using configured private/public key PEM values
- Access token claims include:
  - token id
  - subject
  - issuer
  - audience
  - issued/expiration timestamps
  - token type
  - session id
  - Google subject
  - email
  - placeholder roles/scopes
- Refresh token claims include:
  - token id
  - subject
  - issuer
  - audience
  - issued/expiration timestamps
  - token type
  - session id

## Current User Strategy
- Authentication is limited to pre-registered users in `local` and PostgreSQL-backed users in non-local profiles
- `PreRegisteredUsersProperties` holds the local allow-list
- A user is considered valid only if their Google subject matches and they are active

## Guardrails for Future Changes
- Keep controllers thin
- Keep business rules out of DTOs and adapters
- Avoid Spring annotations in domain entities unless required
- Prefer throwing meaningful exceptions and letting global error handling format them
- Keep token/session policy changes isolated to use cases, domain ports, and adapters

## Testing Guidance
- Controller tests for HTTP contract
- Use case tests for orchestration and rules
- Adapter tests for Google verification, JWT behavior, Redis TTL/session behavior, and in-memory session storage

## Checklist for Adding a New Auth Operation
1. Define request/response DTOs in `presentation/dto`
2. Add command model in `application/command`
3. Implement use case in `application/usecase`
4. Extend domain entities/rules if required
5. Add or extend repository/service ports in `domain/repository`
6. Implement adapter/configuration in `infrastructure`
7. Expose endpoint in `AuthController`
8. Add tests in the corresponding `src/test/.../features/auth/...` layer

## Current Known Gaps
- Roles/permissions and `/me` endpoint remain future work
- Logout-all remains future work
- Audit/logging integration still depends on the wider application’s observability strategy
- Persistence strategy for the rest of the auth ecosystem can still evolve

## Open Decisions to Resolve
- RBAC model and claims strategy
- Logout-all semantics
- Audit requirements for login failures and suspicious activity
- Whether to move more auth metadata to PostgreSQL later

